{"verdict": "reject", "reason": "The second task sets the service user's password to an empty string ('') rather than a locked value like '!' or '*'. The user module then runs `usermod -p ''`, which leaves the shadow hash empty. That makes the account passwordless instead of disabling its password, and can allow login with no password where PAM uses nullok. In the baseline state, the unlocked hash is replaced with an empty one. In the adversarial state, the existing locked hash '!...' differs from '', so it is overwritten and the account ends up with an empty password instead of staying disabled. The requirement to disable the password fails in both states."}
